#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083126 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/id #uuid "cdd74ee4-b82f-520f-8541-f328f9e63fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8279" :question/problem "The birth weights of newborns in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $3\\,543$ grams and a standard deviation of $609$ grams. What is the probability that a randomly selected newborn baby weighs more than $3\\,100$ grams but less than $3\\,600$? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.03 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -0.8 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2033 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -0.7 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2327 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -0.6 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.2643 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.08 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.09 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.10 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -0.1 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.4207 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\phantom{-}0.0 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.5319 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.5359 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, \\phantom{-}0.1 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.5714 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.5753 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the weight of a randomly selected baby. Then we have $X \\sim N(3\\,543, 609^2).$\n\nWe want to find $P(3\\,100 <   X <  3\\,600).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\,100<X<3\\,600) & =P(\\frac{3\\,100-3\\,543}{609}<Z<\\frac{3\\,600-3\\,543}{609}) \\\\  & =P(-0.73<Z<0.09)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.73<Z<0.09) & =P(Z<0.09)-P(Z<-0.73) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.09)-P(Z\\le -0.73) \\\\  & =Φ(0.09)-Φ(-0.73)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.73) & =0.2327 \\\\ Φ(0.09) & =0.5359\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.73<Z<0.09) & =Φ(0.09)-Φ(-0.73) \\\\  & =0.5359-0.2327 \\\\  & =0.3032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the probability that a randomly selected newborn baby weighs more than $3\\,100$ grams but less than $3\\,600$ is $0.30.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083127 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/id #uuid "5905ffa9-e7e2-5288-8714-91357fb17139" :question/math-academy-id "e-8280" :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the episodes of a popular podcast is $43\\,\\textrm{min}.$ Additionally, $20\\%$ of the episodes have a duration of less than $38$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of a randomly selected episode is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the episode durations, rounded to the nearest minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ | $\\, 0.15 \\,$ | $\\, 0.20 \\,$ | $\\, 0.25 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, -2.3263 \\,$ | $\\, -1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, -1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, -1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, -1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, -0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, -0.6745 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly selected episode. Then, we have $X \\sim N(43, \\sigma^2),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma.$ \n\nWe know that $P(X \\leq 38) = 0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 38) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{38-43}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{5}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.20) =-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1683 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.1683} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈6\\,\\text{minutes.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083128 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "4049eb36-a8b0-5ef7-bf82-6f38cbe5cb17" :question/math-academy-id "e-8281" :question/problem "The systolic blood pressure of a group of people is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $12\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ Additionally, $50\\%$ of the people have a systolic blood pressure between $0.93\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and $1.07\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$. Find, as a percentage, the proportion of people with a systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.75 \\,$ | $\\, 0.80 \\,$ | $\\, 0.85 \\,$ | $\\, 0.90 \\,$ | $\\, 0.95 \\,$ | $\\, 0.97 \\,$ | $\\, 0.99 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, 0.6745 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, 1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, 1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, 1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, 1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, 2.3263 \\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p = \\Phi(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6026 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6406 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the systolic blood pressure of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X \\sim N(\\mu, 12^2),$ and we want to find $P(X <  120).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu.$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.93\\mu  <   X <   1.07\\mu ) = 0.5.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.93\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.25 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5+0.25 \\\\ P(X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.75 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12}) & =0.75 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12}) & =0.75 \\\\ \\frac{0.07\\mu }{12} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.75).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.75) \\approx 0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12} & =0.6745 \\\\ 0.07\\mu  & =8.094 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.094}{0.07} \\\\  & ≈115.629.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $P(X <   120)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<120) & =P(Z<\\frac{120-115.629}{12}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.36) \\\\  & =Φ(0.36) \\\\  & =0.6406 \\\\  & ≈64.1%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $64.1\\%$ of the people have systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083342 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"}] :question/id #uuid "c55db5a3-2d8c-57d7-b860-8628cb8738f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9892" :question/problem "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?\n\n![](images/d4/d40721092d0e11fdc2ac582688491a922bd0c6240e5bfa44b6374a73f6577685.png)" :question/worked-solution "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is shown in diagram II:\n\n![](images/38/382e3bc616bc15ac99fb5f18f1f9a75925eb2b5760152c7088fdbc7047fff904.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7836d0df-6d0e-53c1-9b87-066f993123a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493572} {:answer/id #uuid "7f62054b-c721-5065-baa7-a2e088063d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493589} {:answer/id #uuid "ad72aa07-660d-51b0-afc8-b41ca13de413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493664} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4692a1-b2be-56d2-9388-d0e19d2609ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493667} {:answer/id #uuid "b79552a7-3376-5da8-91ca-17bece0d388e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad72aa07-660d-51b0-afc8-b41ca13de413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493664} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d04840-0a76-5834-bd32-257991f12cc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e312f711-a596-58e6-bba4-4f3bc71c5f9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132434" :question/problem "The volume of soda in cans filled at a particular factory is normally distributed with a mean of $12$ ounces and a standard deviation of $0.1$ ounces. What is the probability that a randomly selected soda can contains more than $12.2$ ounces? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the volume (in ounces) of soda in a randomly selected can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{0.1}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X>12.2).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>12.2) & =P(Z>\\frac{12.2-12}{0.1}) \\\\  & =P(Z>2) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(2)=1-0.9772≈0.02.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected soda can contains more than $12.2$ ounces is approximately $0.02.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04822258-26f6-53c8-944e-465c474e11aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493396} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7dcc06-7bd8-5a37-9726-5c0772337fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493495} {:answer/id #uuid "3da7662f-51f3-5819-88a6-da0d21cda61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493496} {:answer/id #uuid "55290d0d-b20c-5db9-a359-89cdf3c54ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493522} {:answer/id #uuid "8afaceac-1ebe-56aa-94bf-2f83f4de4163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04822258-26f6-53c8-944e-465c474e11aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493396} :answer-field/id #uuid "553ac2e3-4992-5bf9-a927-8dd39d781081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4286abc9-bbfc-5418-a3f8-c44338b9b405" :question/math-academy-id "q-133055" :question/problem "The output voltage of a particular model of laptop AC adapters is $19$ volts on average. However, $1%$ of adapters have a voltage greater than $20$ volts. Assuming that the output voltage is normally distributed, find the percent of AC adapters with a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the output voltage of a randomly selected AC adapter. Then we have $X∼N(19,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>19.3).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>20)=0.01.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =1-P(X>20) \\\\  & =1-0.01 \\\\  & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.99 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-19}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)≈2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =2.3263 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{2.32639} \\\\  & ≈0.42985.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>19.3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>19.3) & =1-P(X\\le 19.3) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{19.3-19}{0.42985}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le 0.70) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.70) \\\\  & =1-0.7580 \\\\  & ≈24.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $24.2%$ of AC adapters have a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083342 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2567e0e-29b0-57bd-848a-32c7d8078a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/9969bd82c4b8c2e07acb41cea190ce56c93880a16dc5e4f118f6a7413585906a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388607} {:answer/id #uuid "10c3aec0-6688-53cc-8287-5794de7509f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/47d0812e7cb8b459df66a9d8f153232d10b5cd4532da4787dab0629b7ac0a1a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388608} {:answer/id #uuid "db500a0b-5dfd-5d12-9a7e-0388b2ebf6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/60/60b78b17a9fcad64ad3ec66ab29fe4da750740af4a1258b269597d0500488cc5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388609} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfb9fcb-b239-5df8-9af8-871f42370220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f2/f24b8b76970891cd8a3b8f797f608ff916dd6f05e5e8afddbb5575778b024be7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388610} {:answer/id #uuid "631b4cca-5bfd-50b0-b342-d2b909e8276f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3a73e3c8343526ceb755d6a0b44ba83d816265c5893a94d82a87f8f04ad4969a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdfb9fcb-b239-5df8-9af8-871f42370220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f2/f24b8b76970891cd8a3b8f797f608ff916dd6f05e5e8afddbb5575778b024be7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388610} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6a7ad8-1c7c-51b3-b1e8-1174655fdfab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c34e831-6b73-5cf1-b4f7-78b87586c856" :question/math-academy-id "q-139110" :question/problem "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" :question/worked-solution "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is